# .github/upstream-compat.env
#
# Which upstream OpenShell release the next weekly upstream-sync moves the
# whole pin set to: the crates (Cargo.toml), the chart's upstream.version and its
# gateway, supervisor and sandbox runtime digests. They are ONE release: the
# driver links that release's crates, and scripts/check-image-digests.sh holds
# the digests to upstream.version. The sync's detect job compares the pins
# against this release and its sync moves them to it; it never moves the crates
# backwards, so a release older than the one Cargo.toml pins changes nothing.
# The smokes do not read it: they install the chart's own pins.
#
#   latest    Track bleeding edge. This is the default and the intended
#             steady state.
#   vN.N.NN   Pin. The remedy when a new upstream release is broken (the sync
#             PR that proposes it is red): the next sync then moves the whole
#             set to this release instead. Revert as soon as upstream is fixed.
#
# `latest` resolves to the newest upstream semver RELEASE TAG, then to an
# immutable digest. It is never the mutable `:latest` container tag — that
# tag moved twice in one week and silently handed every new sandbox an
# unreviewed privileged supervisor binary.
#
# Changing this value is a reviewable commit on purpose: it records that
# upstream was broken during a window, instead of leaving it in someone's
# memory. See docs/internal/runbook-upstream-sync.md.
GATEWAY_REF=latest

# PIN_REASON and PIN_REVIEW_AFTER only mean something while GATEWAY_REF is
# pinned to a version, not `latest`. They exist because a pin is silent
# once it's in place: while pinned, the sync's target is the pinned release
# (scripts/check-upstream-pin.sh, resolve-upstream-refs.sh), so the weekly
# detect job has nothing newer to compare against and stays green forever.
# Nothing else notices the pin can be lifted, so the reason it went in has to
# be written down, or it won't be reconstructable months later.
# scripts/check-pin-status.sh reads both of these to report whether the pin
# still holds.
#
#   PIN_REASON        Why upstream is broken and this pin exists. Free text.
#   PIN_REVIEW_AFTER   YYYY-MM-DD. A date past which the pin is worth a
#                      second look, not a hard deadline.
#
# Fill both in in the same commit that sets GATEWAY_REF to a version --
# a pin recorded without its reason is how the reason gets lost.
PIN_REASON=
PIN_REVIEW_AFTER=
